---
import TextPage from '~/layouts/TextPage.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '~/config/site';

const path = '/artwork-guidelines/';
---

<TextPage
  title="Artwork Guidelines | Cone Sleeves UK"
  description="File formats, bleed, colour and die line requirements for supplying artwork for printed cone sleeves and food packaging."
  path={path}
  h1="Artwork Guidelines"
  standfirst="What to send us, how to set it up, and the handful of things that cause almost every artwork problem."
  updated="2026-08-13"
  crumbLabel="Artwork Guidelines"
>
  <h2>Ask for the Die Line First</h2>
  <p>
    This is the single most useful thing on this page. Every product we print has a flat
    shape that is not a rectangle, and a cone sleeve's flat shape is a curved fan.
  </p>
  <p>
    We supply an editable vector template showing the cut line, any folds, the overlap area
    and the safe margin. Ask for it at enquiry stage and give it to whoever designs your
    artwork before they start. Artwork laid out on a rectangle and adjusted afterwards is
    where most problems begin.
  </p>

  <h2>File Formats</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Preferred:</strong> vector PDF, or the native Illustrator file, with the die
      line on its own layer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Accepted:</strong> EPS, SVG, or high-resolution PSD and TIFF where the design
      is genuinely photographic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Please avoid:</strong> Word documents, PowerPoint, screenshots, and images
      pulled from a website or a social media post.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Send the working file rather than a flattened export where you can. Small adjustments
    then take minutes instead of being impossible.
  </p>

  <h2>Resolution</h2>
  <p>
    Any raster element should be at least 300 dpi at final printed size. An image that
    looks sharp on a screen is often around 72 dpi, which is a quarter of what print needs
    and cannot be recovered by enlarging it.
  </p>

  <h2>Bleed and Safe Area</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Bleed:</strong> extend any background colour or image past the cut line on
      every edge. Cutting has a small tolerance, and artwork stopping exactly at the line
      produces a white sliver on some units.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Safe area:</strong> keep anything that matters — logos, type, barcodes — inside
      the safe margin shown on the template.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Overlap:</strong> the glued or tucked area is hidden on every assembled unit.
      Nothing important should sit there.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Colour</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Supply process artwork in CMYK. Files supplied in RGB have to be converted, and the
      conversion shifts colours.
    </li>
    <li>
      Name any critical colour as a Pantone reference rather than a CMYK build or a hex
      code. A named spot colour holds far more consistently across a run.
    </li>
    <li>
      Build large solid blacks as a rich black from more than one ink. A single-ink black
      laid thinly across a big area reads warm and patchy.
    </li>
    <li>
      Expect a shift on kraft and uncoated stocks. Inks are transparent, so a warm brown
      base pulls everything towards it and pale tints can disappear.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Type and Fine Detail</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Convert fonts to outlines, or supply the font files with the artwork.</li>
    <li>
      Avoid very small reversed-out type. Ink spreads slightly into board, which closes up
      fine gaps.
    </li>
    <li>
      On a cone sleeve, type that should read level once wrapped has to be set on the die's
      arc, not on a straight baseline.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep barcodes on a flat panel with clear space around them. A barcode across a crease
      or on a curved area scans unreliably.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Leaving Room for Labels</h2>
  <p>
    If a sticker is applied after printing — an allergen label, a weight, a date code, a
    batch number — reserve a clear area of the right size in the artwork. Deciding this
    afterwards is how a label ends up across a logo.
  </p>
  <p>
    Panels that are written on by hand also need to be left unlaminated, because pen does
    not take on a laminated surface.
  </p>

  <h2>What You Are Confirming</h2>
  <p>
    You are responsible for the content of the artwork you supply: that you hold the rights
    to use it, and that any wording on it is accurate. That includes food labelling,
    ingredient statements and any environmental or recycling claim printed on the pack.
    Packaging can carry whatever you specify; the specification is yours to stand behind.
  </p>

  <h2>The Proof</h2>
  <p>
    We issue a digital proof showing your artwork on the die line. It confirms position,
    content and spelling — not colour, which a screen cannot represent accurately.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before approving, print the flat at 100 per cent on any desktop printer, cut around the
    die line and wrap it round your actual product. Five minutes there catches more than
    any amount of on-screen checking, and every problem found at that stage is free to fix.
  </p>

  <h2>No Artwork Yet?</h2>
  <p>
    A logo is a perfectly good starting point. A single mark repeated around a plain board
    is one of the cheapest and most effective specifications available, and it needs no
    illustration work. Send what you have and we will tell you what will and will not hold
    at print size.
  </p>
  <p>
    Questions about a specific file are quickest by email — send it to
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.email}`}>{CONTACT.email}</a> and we will look at it before you
    commit to anything.
  </p>
</TextPage>
